add(1407,'purpose','Purpose of a map','Which choice best describes the role of a robot map in this lecture?', ['Represent relevant environmental information for tasks such as planning and relocalization','Replace the robot pose estimate with raw motor-current readings','Store only the next wheel command without environmental information','Represent only the robot joint configuration without its surroundings'], 'A map represents environmental information in a form useful for the robot’s task.', ['Motor current alone is not the environmental representation described.','A single command does not represent the surroundings.','Joint configuration describes the robot rather than the surrounding world.'])
add(1408,'occupancy','Occupancy-grid meaning','What does a cell value in the described occupancy grid represent?', ['The probability that an obstacle occupies that cell','The probability that the robot chooses the left action','The index of the nearest named room','The orientation of the robot’s steering joint'], 'An occupancy grid assigns obstacle-occupancy information to spatial cells.', ['Action probability belongs to a policy, not the cell occupancy variable.','Room indices belong to a connectivity representation.','Steering orientation is robot state, not obstacle occupancy.'])
add(1408,'representations','Choosing a map representation','A warehouse robot can reliably detect a small set of poles and markers and needs a compact metric map; which listed representation fits?', ['A feature map containing landmark positions','A raw dense point cloud retaining every measured surface point','A room-connectivity graph without landmark geometry','A raster containing only the last steering commands'], 'A feature map stores a compact set of detectable landmarks and their positions.', ['A dense point cloud may work but does not exploit the requested compact landmark representation.','Connectivity alone omits the requested landmark positions.','Control commands are not the stated feature map.'],typ='scenario',role='premise')
add(1408,'topology','Topological versus geometric detail','What can a bare topological map directly describe without adding metric attributes?', ['Which places are connected by traversable links','The exact distance from every pixel to an obstacle','The complete 3D surface mesh of each room','The occupancy probability of every square centimeter'], 'Nodes and edges express connectivity; a bare graph need not provide detailed metric geometry.', ['Pixel-to-obstacle distances require additional geometry and calibration.','A surface mesh is a different representation.','Cellwise occupancy requires a spatial grid rather than connectivity alone.'],assumptions=['Bare means the graph has not been augmented with metric edge weights or coordinates.'])
add(1408,'hybrid','Hybrid map roles','Why might a robot use a point cloud together with a coarse occupancy grid?', ['Use detailed 3D observations for perception and a simpler spatial representation for planning','Use the grid as camera calibration and the point cloud as wheel-speed control','Use the point cloud only as room names and the grid only as speech labels','Use both solely to duplicate identical map data at identical resolution'], 'The representations can serve different tasks instead of requiring one representation to do everything.', ['These are not the map roles described.','Point clouds and occupancy grids contain spatial information, not only names or speech labels.','The example combines complementary detail and computational costs, not identical duplicates.'])
add(1409,'ideal-state','State under ideal localization','With M 2D landmarks and a perfectly known robot pose, what is the estimated mapping-state dimension?', ['2M','3+2M','M','3M'], 'The map-only state contains two coordinates per landmark and excludes the known robot pose.', ['Three additional pose components belong to planar SLAM.','One value per landmark is insufficient for unrestricted 2D positions.','Three per landmark would describe a different representation.'],typ='definition')
add(1409,'empty-map','Initial map state','Before any landmark has been observed in the ideal mapping example, what are the map-state and covariance sizes?', ['Length 0 and 0×0','Length 3 and 3×3','Length 20 and 20×20','Length 40 and 40×40'], 'The estimator begins with an empty landmark map and adds two values for each newly observed landmark.', ['A three-value pose state is not the map-only initial state.','The true simulated number of landmarks does not prepopulate the estimator.','Forty would describe twenty already estimated 2D landmarks, not the empty map.'])
add(1410,'static-prediction','Static-landmark prediction','If landmarks are static and no map process noise is added, how do their predicted mean and covariance change before a new observation?', ['Both remain unchanged','The mean remains unchanged but covariance must double','The covariance remains unchanged but all means move with the robot','Both become zero after each robot movement'], 'The static identity transition with zero process noise leaves the existing landmark mean and covariance unchanged.', ['Covariance growth requires a specified uncertainty contribution.','World-fixed landmarks do not move merely because the robot moves.','Zeroing the state would erase the map rather than predict it.'])
add(1410,'inverse-model','Converting a range-bearing observation','A known robot pose is (2 m, 3 m, 90°); a landmark is measured at range 4 m and bearing −90° relative to the robot; where is it in world coordinates?', ['(6 m, 3 m)','(2 m, 7 m)','(2 m, −1 m)','(−2 m, 3 m)'], 'The world bearing is 90°−90°=0°, so the observation adds (4,0) to (2,3).', ['This neglects the relative bearing.','This treats the relative bearing as the world bearing.','This reverses the range direction.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Add heading and relative bearing to obtain 0°.','Compute displacement (4 cos 0°, 4 sin 0°)=(4,0) m.','Add robot translation to obtain (6,3) m.'],forms=[formula('p = [x_v+r*cos(theta+beta), y_v+r*sin(theta+beta)]',{'p':'world landmark position','x_v,y_v':'robot position','r':'range','theta':'robot heading','beta':'relative bearing'},{'p':'m','x_v,y_v':'m','r':'m','theta,beta':'consistent angular unit'})])
add([1409,1410],'augmentation-size','Dimensionally consistent map augmentation','A map-only estimator already has M 2D landmarks and adds one new landmark; what is the new covariance size?', ['(2M+2)×(2M+2)','(M+2)×(M+2)','(2M+3)×(2M+3)','(2M)×(2M)'], 'The old state has 2M coordinates and the new landmark adds two, so every covariance dimension is 2M+2.', ['This counts one rather than two coordinates per old landmark.','Three new values would incorrectly add a planar pose.','The covariance must expand with the new state coordinates.'],role='conflict',conflict='The slide’s augmentation blocks mix M as a landmark count with M as a state dimension; this question explicitly uses M 2D landmarks.')
add([1410,1411],'known-pose-jacobian','Known pose versus a zero derivative','For g=(x_v+r cos(theta+beta), y_v+r sin(theta+beta)), what does a perfectly known robot pose imply?', ['Its covariance contribution is zero, but the derivative of g with respect to pose is generally nonzero','All derivatives of g with respect to pose are zero because the pose is known','The measurement covariance is zero because the pose is known','The landmark coordinates do not depend on the robot translation'], 'Knowing a variable exactly removes its uncertainty contribution, not the mathematical dependence of the inverse model on that variable.', ['Translation derivatives remain one and heading derivatives generally remain nonzero.','A noisy range-bearing sensor can remain uncertain even with exact pose.','Both robot translation coordinates explicitly appear in g.'],role='conflict',conflict='The slide prints Gx=∂g/∂x_v as an all-zero matrix in ideal localization; the derivative itself is nonzero.')
add(1411,'measurement-jacobian','Inverse-observation uncertainty directions','At world bearing theta+beta=0 and range r=4 m, what is Gz=∂g/∂(r,beta), using radians for beta?', ['[[1,0],[0,4]]','[[0,−4],[1,0]]','[[4,0],[0,1]]','[[1,0],[0,1]]'], 'At zero world bearing, range changes world x by one per meter and bearing changes world y by r per radian.', ['This is the Jacobian at a 90° world bearing.','This swaps the range and angular sensitivities.','The bearing sensitivity must include the range 4.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Use cos 0=1 and sin 0=0.','Substitute into [[cos a,−r sin a],[sin a,r cos a]].','Obtain [[1,0],[0,4]].'],forms=[formula('Gz = [[cos a,-r*sin a],[sin a,r*cos a]]',{'a':'theta+beta','r':'measured range'},{'a':'rad','r':'m'})])
add([1410,1411],'new-landmark-cov','Covariance of a new landmark','An exact pose observes r=4 m at world bearing 0, with independent range variance 0.01 m² and bearing variance 0.0004 rad²; what is the new landmark covariance?', ['diag(0.01,0.0064) m²','diag(0.01,0.0004) m²','diag(0.16,0.0004) m²','diag(0.1,0.08) m²'], 'Gz=diag(1,4), so Gz W Gzᵀ scales the bearing variance by 4², giving 0.0064 m².', ['This omits the range-squared factor in the transverse variance.','This scales the range component instead of the angular component.','These are standard deviations rather than the requested variances.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Use Gz=diag(1,4).','Compute the first variance as 1²×0.01=0.01.','Compute the second variance as 4²×0.0004=0.0064.'],forms=[formula('P_new = Gz*W*Gz^T',{'P_new':'landmark covariance','Gz':'inverse measurement Jacobian','W':'measurement covariance'},{'P_new':'m²','W':'range/bearing squared units'})],assumptions=['Pose uncertainty is exactly zero.','Range and bearing errors are independent.'])
add(1411,'bearing-quadrant','Correct bearing quadrant','A robot at (0,0) faces world +x and sees a landmark at (−1,1); which bearing function gives the correct 135° quadrant?', ['atan2(1,−1)','atan(1/−1)','atan2(−1,1)','atan(−1/1)'], 'atan2(dy,dx) uses both coordinate signs and returns 135° for the second quadrant.', ['The ratio alone gives −45° and loses quadrant information.','Swapping the arguments returns −45°.','This also yields −45° rather than the required second quadrant.'],typ='numerical',role='conflict',diff='medium',conflict='The slide writes arctan(dy/dx), which needs quadrant handling; this item explicitly uses atan2.',steps=['Compute dx=−1 and dy=1.','Recognize a second-quadrant vector.','Use atan2(dy,dx)=135°.'])
add(1411,'bearing-residual','Wrapping an angular innovation','Predicted bearing is 179° and observed bearing is −179°; what is the shortest signed observed-minus-predicted innovation in (−180°,180°]?', ['+2°','−358°','−2°','+358°'], 'Raw subtraction gives −358°, which is equivalent to +2° after adding 360°.', ['This is the unwrapped difference, not the requested shortest angular residual.','This reverses observed-minus-predicted order.','This is neither the raw subtraction nor its shortest equivalent.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Subtract 179° from −179° to get −358°.','Add 360° to enter the stated interval.','Use +2° as the innovation.'],assumptions=['Angles represent the same circular bearing variable.'])
add(1411,'observation-jacobian','Landmark observation derivative','For a robot at the origin and a landmark at (3,4), what is the range/bearing Jacobian with respect to landmark (x,y)?', ['[[3/5,4/5],[−4/25,3/25]]','[[3/25,4/25],[−4/5,3/5]]','[[3/5,4/5],[4/25,−3/25]]','[[1,0],[0,1]]'], 'The range is 5; its derivatives are dx/r and dy/r, while bearing derivatives are −dy/r² and dx/r².', ['The denominators for range and bearing are interchanged.','The bearing derivative signs are reversed.','Cartesian coordinates are not identical to range and bearing.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Compute r=sqrt(3²+4²)=5.','Use range row (3/5,4/5).','Use bearing row (−4/25,3/25).'])
add(1411,'sparse-h','Observed-landmark block in H','In ideal map-only localization, an observation of landmark i directly depends on which landmark-state blocks?', ['The block for landmark i, with zero direct derivatives for other landmarks','Every landmark block with identical nonzero derivatives','Only the most recently appended landmark, regardless of identity','No landmark block because robot pose is known'], 'The local observation model uses the selected landmark position, so other landmark blocks have zero direct observation derivatives.', ['Cross-covariance can propagate corrections, but it does not create direct observation dependence on all landmarks.','The observed identity determines the block, not insertion order.','Known robot pose does not make the landmark observation independent of landmark position.'])
add(1411,'innovation','Measurement innovation meaning','What quantity is multiplied by the Kalman gain in the EKF correction?', ['Observed measurement minus predicted measurement','Observed measurement minus predicted state coordinates','Predicted covariance minus measurement covariance','Observed range divided by the number of landmarks'], 'The innovation is a residual in measurement space, z−h(predicted state), with angular residuals handled consistently.', ['State and measurement vectors can have different meanings and dimensions.','Covariance differences are not measurement residuals.','Landmark count is not part of the innovation definition.'])
add(1412,'true-map-vs-estimator','True simulation map versus estimator input','In the mapping screenshot, RangeBearingSensor receives map, but EKF is constructed without a map argument; what does this distinction mean?', ['The simulator knows the world to generate observations while the estimator builds an unknown map','The EKF is automatically given every landmark coordinate through its map argument','The sensor measures robot odometry instead of landmarks','The estimator must use no range-bearing observations'], 'The sensor’s ground-truth map generates observations; omitting the EKF map input selects map estimation rather than known-map localization.', ['No map argument is supplied to this EKF call.','The sensor remains a range-bearing landmark sensor.','The EKF receives the sensor and its covariance tuple.'],code='EKF problem-selection table: map creation uses robot=(veh,None), sensor=(smodel,W), map=None.')
add(1412,'variance-squaring','Covariance from standard deviations','The code sets W=np.diag([0.1, np.deg2rad(1)])**2; what is the range variance?', ['0.01 m²','0.1 m²','0.2 m²','1 m²'], 'The range standard deviation 0.1 m is squared to obtain variance 0.01 m².', ['This retains the standard deviation’s numeric value rather than squaring it.','Squaring does not mean multiplying by two.','The one-degree angular input is not the range variance.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',steps=['Read the range standard deviation 0.1 m.','Compute 0.1²=0.01 m².'])
add(1412,'sensor-window','Finite sensor visibility','With sensor range 4 m and bearing interval [−π/2,π/2], which landmark is inside both limits?', ['Range 3 m, bearing 60°','Range 5 m, bearing 60°','Range 3 m, bearing 120°','Range 5 m, bearing 120°'], 'Three meters is within 4 m and 60° lies in the frontal ±90° interval.', ['Its range exceeds the limit.','Its bearing exceeds the frontal interval.','Both its range and bearing exceed the limits.'],typ='scenario',role='premise',assumptions=['No additional occlusion or sampling restrictions are applied in this question.'])
add(1413,'partial-observation','Interpreting the simulated estimated map','Why can a simulation with twenty true landmarks finish with fewer than twenty estimated landmarks?', ['Only observed landmarks are added, and the robot may not observe every landmark','An EKF always removes exactly half the landmarks by definition','Each landmark is stored as two separate map objects instead of coordinates','A true landmark count is itself the sensor bearing limit'], 'Limited range, field of view and the traveled path can leave true landmarks unobserved.', ['There is no fixed half-map deletion rule.','Two coordinates represent one 2D landmark, not two observed objects.','Landmark count and bearing limits are different quantities.'])
add(1414,'problem-types','Localization, mapping and SLAM','Which task estimates both the robot pose and the previously unknown map?', ['SLAM','Known-map localization alone','Ideal-pose mapping alone','Open-loop odometry integration alone'], 'Simultaneous localization and mapping jointly estimates pose and map.', ['Known-map localization assumes map information is already supplied.','Ideal-pose mapping assumes the robot pose is known.','Odometry alone does not infer an unknown environmental map.'],typ='definition')
add(1415,'coupling','Why localization and mapping are coupled','Why is SLAM described as a chicken-and-egg problem?', ['A map helps estimate pose, while pose helps place observations in the map','A robot must finish mapping before it can collect any sensor data','A robot must know all future poses before observing one landmark','A map and pose estimate use unrelated measurements'], 'The two unknowns inform each other, motivating joint or coupled estimation.', ['Sensor data can be collected while both quantities remain uncertain.','Future poses need not be known to observe a landmark.','SLAM exploits their shared observations rather than treating them as unrelated.'])
add(1416,'joint-dimension','Planar SLAM state size','A planar EKF-SLAM estimator has eight 2D landmarks; how long is its state vector?', ['19','16','24','11'], 'It has three robot-pose components plus sixteen landmark coordinates: 3+2×8=19.', ['Sixteen omits the robot pose.','Twenty-four incorrectly assigns three values to each landmark without the stated state definition.','Eleven counts only one coordinate per landmark.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',steps=['Count x, y and heading as three pose values.','Count eight pairs as sixteen landmark values.','Add to obtain nineteen.'])
add(1416,'cross-block','Robot-map covariance shape','For M 2D landmarks, what is the size of the covariance block from robot pose to map coordinates?', ['3×2M','2M×3','3×3','M×M'], 'Rows correspond to the three pose variables and columns to the 2M map coordinates.', ['This is the transpose map-to-robot block.','This is the robot-only covariance block.','This does not count two coordinates per landmark or the pose variables.'])
add(1417,'correlated-update','An observation can update another landmark','How can observing landmark A change the estimate of unobserved landmark B in joint EKF-SLAM?', ['Existing cross-covariance can give B a nonzero gain for A’s observation','The observation directly measures every landmark’s position','The filter must physically move B to preserve the robot pose','B’s update must have the same magnitude and sign as A’s'], 'A nonzero covariance connection can propagate information to B through the joint Kalman gain.', ['The direct observation model still concerns A.','Only estimates are updated; static objects are not moved by the filter.','Gain magnitudes and signs depend on the covariance structure.'],assumptions=['The relevant cross-covariance is nonzero; an uncorrelated component need not change.'])
add(1417,'pose-jacobian','Pose uncertainty in new-landmark placement','Which derivative pair describes the heading column of the inverse-observation pose Jacobian?', ['(−r sin(theta+beta), r cos(theta+beta))ᵀ','(r cos(theta+beta), r sin(theta+beta))ᵀ','(1,1)ᵀ','(0,0)ᵀ for every heading and range'], 'Differentiate the cosine and sine position terms with respect to heading.', ['This is the radial displacement direction multiplied by r, not the heading derivative.','The heading sensitivity is not a constant translation in both axes.','Nonzero range generally gives a nonzero heading derivative.'],typ='derivation',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Differentiate r cos(theta+beta) to −r sin(theta+beta).','Differentiate r sin(theta+beta) to r cos(theta+beta).'])
add(1418,'initial-covariance','Converting initial uncertainties','In the 1D example, independent robot and landmark standard deviations are 0.5 m and 1 m; what is their initial covariance matrix?', ['diag(0.25,1) m²','diag(0.5,1) m²','diag(0.25,0.5) m²','[[0.25,0.5],[0.5,1]] m²'], 'Square each standard deviation and use zero cross-covariance under the stated independence.', ['The robot standard deviation was not squared.','The landmark variance is 1²=1 rather than 0.5.','A nonzero cross term contradicts the stated independence.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',steps=['Compute 0.5²=0.25.','Compute 1²=1.','Use zero off-diagonal entries.'])
add([1418,1419],'one-d-predict','Predicting the 1D SLAM state','Starting with mean (2,5) m and covariance diag(0.25,1) m², add robot odometry +1 m with independent variance 0.25 m² and keep the landmark static; what is predicted?', ['Mean (3,5) m and covariance diag(0.5,1) m²','Mean (3,6) m and covariance diag(0.5,1.25) m²','Mean (3,5) m and covariance diag(0.25,1) m²','Mean (2,5) m and covariance diag(0.5,1) m²'], 'Only the robot mean advances, and independent robot motion variance adds to its prior variance.', ['The landmark is static and has no process-noise increment here.','This omits the stated robot motion uncertainty.','This omits the stated odometry increment.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Add 1 to robot mean 2, giving 3.','Keep landmark mean 5.','Add 0.25+0.25=0.5 to robot variance and keep landmark variance 1.'])
add(1419,'signed-range','One-dimensional measurement model','For the example’s signed measurement h=x_landmark−x_robot, what is H with state order (x_robot,x_landmark)?', ['[−1,1]','[1,−1]','[1,1]','[0,1]'], 'Increasing robot position decreases the signed separation; increasing landmark position increases it.', ['This reverses the subtraction order.','The two derivative signs are not both positive.','The measurement depends on robot position as well as landmark position.'],assumptions=['This is signed separation, with the landmark ahead in the example; an absolute-range model needs sign handling.'])
add([1419,1420],'innovation-variance','Innovation variance in the 1D update','Given P=diag(0.5,1), H=[−1,1] and scalar measurement variance R=0.25, what is S=H P Hᵀ+R?', ['1.75','1.25','0.75','1.5'], 'The two state variances contribute 0.5+1, and measurement variance adds 0.25, giving 1.75.', ['This omits the robot variance contribution.','This omits the landmark variance contribution.','This omits measurement variance.'],typ='numerical',role='conflict',diff='medium',conflict='The slide prints a two-element noise Jacobian beside scalar measurement noise; this question explicitly uses the dimensionally consistent scalar noise model.',steps=['Compute H P Hᵀ=(−1)²×0.5+1²×1=1.5.','Add R=0.25.','Obtain S=1.75.'])
add(1420,'one-d-gain','Kalman gain for the 1D measurement','With P=diag(0.5,1), H=[−1,1] and innovation variance S=1.75, what is K=P Hᵀ/S?', ['(−2/7,4/7)ᵀ','(2/7,4/7)ᵀ','(−4/7,2/7)ᵀ','(−1/2,1)ᵀ'], 'P Hᵀ=(−0.5,1)ᵀ; division by 1.75 gives (−2/7,4/7)ᵀ.', ['The robot gain sign must be negative for this signed measurement.','The component magnitudes are interchanged.','This omits division by innovation variance.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Multiply P by Hᵀ to obtain (−0.5,1)ᵀ.','Divide each component by 1.75.','Simplify to (−2/7,4/7)ᵀ.'])
add(1420,'one-d-update','Correcting the joint mean','Predicted mean is (3,5) m, observation is 2.3 m, h=x_landmark−x_robot and K=(−2/7,4/7)ᵀ; what corrected mean results?', ['Approximately (2.9143,5.1714) m','Approximately (3.0857,4.8286) m','Approximately (2.3429,6.3143) m','Approximately (3,5.3) m'], 'Predicted separation is 2 m, so the innovation is 0.3 m; applying both gain components gives the stated joint correction.', ['This reverses the innovation sign.','This multiplies the gain by the raw observation rather than the innovation.','This ignores the computed gain and assigns the entire residual to the landmark.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Compute h=5−3=2 m.','Compute innovation 2.3−2=0.3 m.','Add (−2/7,4/7)×0.3 to (3,5).'])
add(1420,'posterior-cross-cov','Checking the printed covariance','Using P=diag(0.5,1), H=[−1,1] and K=(−2/7,4/7)ᵀ, what is the off-diagonal entry of (I−K H)P?', ['2/7 ≈ 0.2857 m²','1/7 ≈ 0.1429 m²','0 m²','−2/7 ≈ −0.2857 m²'], 'The first row of I−K H is (5/7,2/7), so multiplying by diag(0.5,1) gives the cross entry 2/7.', ['This matches the slide’s approximate 0.143 but not the displayed model’s matrix product.','The shared observation induces nonzero correlation.','The cross entry is positive for the stated H and K.'],typ='numerical',role='conflict',diff='medium',conflict='The printed posterior off-diagonal is 0.143; direct multiplication and the Joseph covariance form both give 2/7≈0.285714.',steps=['Compute I−K H=[[5/7,2/7],[4/7,3/7]].','Multiply the first column by 0.5 and the second by 1.','Obtain [[5/14,2/7],[2/7,3/7]] m².'])
add(1420,'estimate-not-motion','Interpreting a landmark correction','When the 1D correction changes the landmark estimate from 5 m to about 5.1714 m, what moved?', ['The estimated landmark coordinate, not the assumed static landmark','The physical landmark by exactly the innovation','The camera’s coordinate origin by the full measured range','Both objects physically moved by their covariance values'], 'An estimation update changes the belief about location; it is not a motion command or evidence that a static landmark moved.', ['The innovation is a measurement discrepancy, not observed physical displacement of the landmark.','The correction does not redefine the origin by the measured range.','Covariance has squared-distance units and is not physical displacement.'])
add(1421,'slam-configuration','Selecting SLAM in the code','Which change from map-only EKF configuration enables estimation of both vehicle pose and landmarks?', ['Supply the vehicle noise covariance and initial pose covariance, while keeping the EKF map argument absent','Supply the complete map to EKF and remove the sensor','Keep robot=(robot,None) and remove the landmark state','Set the sensor covariance to zero and discard robot motion'], 'The SLAM configuration uses robot=(robot,V), P0 and a sensor, with no known-map argument.', ['Removing observations and providing a known map does not select the displayed joint estimation configuration.','A None vehicle covariance selects the ideal-pose mapping mode.','Zero sensor covariance is not the mode-selection mechanism.'],code='EKF problem-selection table and constructor flags _est_vehicle and _est_ekf_map.')
add(1422,'alignment','What map alignment establishes','What does applying a rigid transformation between estimated and reference maps address in the displayed SLAM comparison?', ['A difference between coordinate frames before comparing corresponding geometry','Every possible local shape distortion in the estimated map','The complete elimination of measurement noise in the filter','Proof that the initial absolute world pose was known from observations'], 'Rigid alignment can reconcile translation and rotation between frames; it does not generally repair local geometric distortions or prove absolute observability.', ['A rigid transform cannot independently deform each landmark location.','Alignment does not remove all measurement noise.','Agreement after alignment does not establish that an absolute reference was observed.'],role='premise')
add(1423,'history-summary','EKF information from earlier observations','Which statement correctly qualifies the comparison of EKF and pose-graph SLAM?', ['An EKF covariance summarizes accumulated evidence, while a pose graph explicitly retains constraints among multiple poses','An EKF discards every earlier observation’s effect after each update','A pose graph always reaches the global optimum regardless of initialization','An EKF covariance stores only the most recent sensor reading'], 'Filtering compresses historical information into the current joint estimate; graph methods represent multiple poses and their constraints explicitly.', ['Earlier information can persist in the mean and covariance.','Nonlinear optimization can depend on initialization and local minima.','A covariance is not simply a copy of one reading.'],role='conflict',conflict='The slide’s most-recent-correlations wording can suggest that EKF forgets prior information; its current joint distribution summarizes past updates.')
add(1424,'graph-edges','Edges in a pose-landmark graph','In the displayed generalized graph, what do odometry and landmark-observation edges constrain?', ['Relative poses and pose-to-landmark relations, respectively','Only landmark colors and only wheel diameters, respectively','Absolute clock time and battery voltage, respectively','Two separate copies of the same landmark count'], 'Odometry relates successive robot poses; observations relate robot poses to environmental landmarks.', ['These quantities are not the edge measurements shown.','The graph does not label these edges as clock or battery constraints.','An edge expresses a measured relation rather than merely a count.'],typ='diagram')
add(1424,'rotation-sign','Checking a planar rotation matrix','Under the standard counterclockwise convention, which matrix represents a rotation by angle a?', ['[[cos a,−sin a],[sin a,cos a]]','[[cos a,sin a],[sin a,cos a]]','[[sin a,−cos a],[cos a,sin a]]','[[cos a,−sin a],[−sin a,cos a]]'], 'The standard rotation sends the +x unit vector to (cos a,sin a) and preserves orthonormal axes with determinant +1.', ['The two positive sine entries generally violate orthogonality.','At a=0 this is a 90° rotation rather than identity.','The two negative sine entries generally violate orthogonality.'],role='conflict',conflict='The slide prints a planar matrix with both off-diagonal sine entries positive; the question states the standard counterclockwise convention.')
add(1424,'relative-transform','Relative pose from world poses','If T_i and T_j map local coordinates into the world frame, which transform maps coordinates in frame j into frame i?', ['T_i⁻¹ T_j','T_j⁻¹ T_i','T_i T_j','T_i⁻¹ T_j⁻¹'], 'First T_j maps from j to world, then T_i⁻¹ maps world coordinates into i.', ['This maps in the reverse direction.','This does not cancel the intermediate world frame in the stated convention.','This reverses both transforms and does not represent the requested composition.'],typ='derivation',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Map a frame-j vector into world coordinates with T_j.','Map the world vector into frame i with T_i⁻¹.','Compose right to left as T_i⁻¹ T_j.'])
add(1424,'zero-residual','Zero geometric residual','If measured relative transform T_z exactly equals T_i⁻¹ T_j, what is the transform residual T_z⁻¹ T_i⁻¹ T_j?', ['The identity transform','The all-zero 3×3 matrix','T_i itself','Twice T_z'], 'Substituting the matching measurement gives T_z⁻¹ T_z=I.', ['A rigid-transform identity represents zero geometric error; a zero matrix is not a valid rigid transform.','The matching measurement cancels the relative transform.','Transform composition is not scalar doubling.'],typ='derivation',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Replace T_i⁻¹ T_j by T_z.','Multiply T_z⁻¹ T_z.','Obtain identity; its corresponding minimal residual vector is zero.'])
add([1424,1426],'residual-vector','From transform residual to quadratic cost','A planar transform residual is a 3×3 matrix; what must be specified before treating fᵀ Ω f as a scalar least-squares edge cost?', ['A consistent minimal residual vector and matching information-matrix dimensions','Only the number of columns in the original camera image','A replacement of every matrix by its largest entry without a stated model','An assumption that every 3×3 matrix is already a scalar'], 'A planar residual can be represented by three coordinate errors; Ω must act on that vector in the same convention.', ['Image width does not define the pose residual coordinates.','Discarding entries arbitrarily does not define the intended geometric error.','Matrix dimensions do not vanish automatically.'],role='conflict',conflict='The slides pass from a transform residual to fᵀΩf without defining the minimal vector representation; this question makes that required convention explicit.')
add(1425,'loop-closure','Information from returning to a landmark','Why does observing an earlier landmark after a loop help correct odometry drift?', ['It adds a relation between poses that were previously connected mainly through accumulated motion estimates','It changes every earlier odometry reading into an exact measurement','It proves all newly observed objects are the same landmark','It removes the need to check landmark identity'], 'A valid loop-closure relation supplies additional consistency information across the traveled loop.', ['The noisy readings are not retroactively made exact.','Loop closure depends on correct correspondence, not automatic identity of every object.','Incorrect landmark association can introduce a false constraint.'])
add(1425,'pose-only-graph','Pure pose graph versus generalized graph','How does a pure pose-graph state differ from the earlier generalized pose-landmark graph?', ['It explicitly optimizes robot poses, with landmark information represented through derived constraints if used','It explicitly contains only landmark positions and no robot poses','It has one scalar state equal to the number of observations','It must contain every image pixel as an independent pose'], 'A pure pose graph has pose variables; landmark observations can help construct constraints without retaining each landmark as a state variable.', ['This describes a landmark-only map, not a pose graph.','Counting observations is not a pose estimate.','Pixels are not themselves robot-pose variables.'],assumptions=['Pure pose graph is distinguished from a graph that retains both pose and landmark nodes.'])
add(1426,'information-weight','Information weighting','Two edges have the same scalar residual 2, with information weights 1 and 4; what are their weighted squared costs?', ['4 and 16','2 and 8','4 and 4','16 and 4'], 'For scalar cost Ω f², squaring residual 2 gives 4, then multiplication by the weights gives 4 and 16.', ['This omits squaring the residual.','This ignores the different confidence weights.','This reverses the two weights.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',steps=['Square residual 2 to obtain 4.','Compute 1×4=4 and 4×4=16.'],forms=[formula('F = f^T*Omega*f',{'f':'residual vector','Omega':'information weight matrix','F':'weighted error cost'},{'F':'dimensionless when information has inverse squared residual units'})])
add(1426,'iteration-plot','Interpreting optimization plots','The pose-graph figure labels iterations 1 through 5; what do these panels primarily show?', ['Successive estimates while optimizing the same graph','Five additional physical laps that the robot must drive','Five independent maps with no shared measurements','Five guaranteed global minima of the same cost'], 'The displayed sequence illustrates iterative refinement of pose estimates for accumulated constraints.', ['An optimization iteration need not require another physical lap.','The panels concern a shared graph being refined.','The illustration does not prove global optimality.'],typ='diagram')
add(1427,'front-back','Front end and back end','Which pairing matches the described SLAM architecture?', ['Front end: create motion/observation constraints; back end: optimize the graph','Front end: optimize all historical poses; back end: capture raw camera pixels only','Front end: compute battery charge; back end: erase landmark associations','Front end: store no measurements; back end: generate arbitrary constraints'], 'The front end interprets sensing and motion into constraints, while the back end solves the resulting estimation problem.', ['This reverses the main roles described.','These are not the illustrated roles.','Constraints must be grounded in observations or motion, not arbitrary generation.'])
add(1427,'sparsity','Why graph structure can be sparse','Why can the pose-graph least-squares system exploit sparsity?', ['Each measurement usually relates a limited subset of state variables','Every measurement directly observes every past pose with equal sensitivity','Every covariance entry must be identically zero','The robot can ignore all loop closures'], 'Local relationships make many direct Jacobian blocks zero, allowing sparse numerical methods.', ['This would create dense direct coupling rather than the stated sparse structure.','Sparsity does not mean all uncertainty or coupling is zero.','Loop closures are valuable constraints and are not excluded by sparse methods.'],role='premise')
add(1429,'resource-motivation','Qualifying the resource argument','Which interpretation of the floor-finding motivation avoids an overbroad claim about SLAM?', ['Some SLAM configurations are costly, so task-specific lightweight alternatives can be useful','All SLAM systems require a dedicated depth camera','No resource-constrained robot can perform any SLAM','Every monocular method has the same compute and sensing requirements'], 'The appropriate method depends on sensors, algorithms, hardware and the task; the motivation is not a universal impossibility result.', ['Monocular SLAM is possible without a dedicated depth camera.','Suitability depends on the particular implementation and resources.','Monocular methods differ in their goals and computational demands.'],role='conflict',conflict='The introduction broadly says SLAM requires depth sensing and is unsuitable for resource-constrained robots; these are not universal requirements.')
add(1429,'sfm','Structure from Motion limitation','Why can a feature-poor scene be difficult for the described Structure from Motion approach?', ['Reliable correspondences and motion parallax can be insufficient for reconstructing scene geometry','A feature-poor scene necessarily has no physical depth','Removing features always makes correspondence uniquely determined','Every image then directly supplies an exact metric 3D mesh'], 'Recovering structure from moving-camera images depends on informative visual observations and correspondences.', ['Texture scarcity does not imply that the world lacks depth.','Fewer distinguishable features generally make matching harder, not unique.','A single image or uninformative sequence does not automatically provide exact metric geometry.'])
add(1430,'floor-reference','The immediate-floor assumption','What assumption supports using the image region immediately in front of the robot as a floor reference?', ['That the sampled region is obstacle-free and representative of the floor appearance','That the sampled region contains all obstacle categories','That every object has a color different from the floor','That the camera has no perspective projection'], 'The reference sample is treated as an example of traversable floor, so contamination or unrepresentative appearance can mislead classification.', ['The reference models floor, not a complete obstacle-class dataset.','Similar colors are one reason to combine multiple visual attributes.','The camera still has perspective and needs calibration for distance estimates.'])
add(1430,'appearance-features','Visual attributes used by Floor Finder','Which feature combination matches the lecture’s floor characterization?', ['Hue, saturation, luminosity and roughness','Latitude, longitude, altitude and satellite clock bias','Wheel torque, gear ratio, motor current and encoder ticks','Only a single named object label per frame'], 'The described method combines color, brightness and texture-related attributes rather than relying on one color value.', ['These are positioning variables, not the listed image-appearance attributes.','These describe actuation and motion sensing rather than image appearance.','The method classifies floor appearance using several visual attributes.'])
add(1430,'texture-qualification','Texture versus color','How should “texture rather than simple color matching” be read alongside the listed hue and saturation features?', ['Texture augments an appearance model that can also use color; it does not imply color features are absent','Hue and saturation are odometry measurements rather than color features','The method uses no image intensity information of any kind','The listed hue and saturation values guarantee identical behavior under all lighting'], 'The slide lists both color and texture attributes, so the distinction is from color-only matching, not a prohibition on color.', ['Hue and saturation describe color.','Luminosity is explicitly included.','Appearance features remain sensitive to conditions and do not establish a universal invariance guarantee.'],role='conflict',conflict='The same slide emphasizes texture rather than color but also lists hue, saturation and luminosity; preserve the combined-feature interpretation.')
add([1430,1432],'roi-contamination','A contaminated reference patch','A toy fills much of the foreground reference region; which failure is most directly possible?', ['The model can learn the toy’s appearance as floor and misclassify similar pixels','The camera focal length must become zero','Every obstacle is guaranteed to be rejected more accurately','The ROI automatically becomes a complete geometric map'], 'The reference assumes floor; contaminating it can corrupt the appearance model used for later classification.', ['Appearance contamination does not physically set focal length.','A contaminated sample does not guarantee better rejection.','An appearance patch is not a complete geometric reconstruction.'],typ='scenario',role='premise')
add(1431,'downsample-count','Pixel-count reduction','How many times fewer pixels are processed when a 640×480 image is downsampled to 320×240?', ['4 times fewer','2 times fewer','8 times fewer','16 times fewer'], 'Both dimensions are halved, so the pixel count is reduced by 2×2=4.', ['This accounts for only one dimension.','This would require an additional factor of two not present here.','This would correspond to quartering each dimension.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',steps=['Compute 640×480=307,200 pixels.','Compute 320×240=76,800 pixels.','Divide 307,200 by 76,800 to obtain 4.'])
add(1431,'downsample-tradeoff','Downsampling trade-off','What trade-off accompanies reducing image resolution before floor classification?', ['Less processing and transfer data, but potentially less detail for small obstacles','Less processing with guaranteed preservation of every one-pixel obstacle','More geometric detail without any new sensor information','A complete removal of the need for camera calibration'], 'Downsampling reduces the number of samples, which can lower cost while losing fine spatial detail.', ['Small structures can disappear or merge during resampling.','Reducing samples does not create additional measured detail.','Metric distance inference still requires the appropriate geometry and calibration.'])
add(1431,'aspect-ratio','Preserving image geometry when resizing','The slide lists 1900×1200 and 320×240 as example resolutions; what should be checked before treating resized coordinates as a uniform-scale camera image?', ['Their aspect ratios differ, so crop, pad or adjusted calibration may be needed','Their aspect ratios are identical because both widths exceed heights','The principal point must always remain at its old pixel coordinates','Only the file name determines whether the geometry changes'], '1900/1200 differs from 320/240; an unqualified resize can scale horizontal and vertical coordinates differently.', ['Both being landscape does not make their ratios equal.','Pixel calibration parameters must be transformed with image geometry.','Geometry depends on the actual image operation.'],role='premise')
add(1431,'blur','Purpose and limitation of Gaussian smoothing','What is the intended effect of the repeated Gaussian blur in the pipeline?', ['Suppress high-frequency variation before classification, while potentially softening small features','Estimate metric obstacle distance without a camera model','Increase all edge contrasts while preserving every fine texture exactly','Convert every non-floor pixel into a guaranteed floor pixel'], 'Smoothing reduces rapid image variation but can also blur boundaries and small objects.', ['Distance requires geometry or calibration, not blur alone.','Low-pass smoothing does not preserve every fine feature or increase all contrasts.','Blur is not a guarantee of correct semantic classification.'])
add(1431,'blur-support','Support of repeated smoothing','Ignoring image borders, three successive one-dimensional convolutions with full five-tap kernels have what maximum input support width?', ['13 pixels','15 pixels','5 pixels','125 pixels'], 'Each additional five-tap convolution expands the support by four: 1+3×(5−1)=13.', ['Adding kernel widths directly double-counts their overlap.','Five describes only one pass.','Multiplying widths counts combinations, not distinct spatial support.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['One pass has width 5.','A second pass expands to 5+4=9.','A third pass expands to 9+4=13.'],assumptions=['This is an author-added support calculation for the slide’s three-pass example.','Kernels have nonzero coefficients at all five positions; ignore borders.'])
add(1432,'threshold','Applying a stated pixel tolerance','For an added scalar example, a reference intensity is 100 and classification accepts absolute differences at most 15; which intensity is accepted?', ['115','116','84','130'], 'The inclusive interval is [85,115], so 115 is accepted.', ['Its difference is 16, outside the interval.','Its difference is 16, outside the interval.','Its difference is 30, outside the interval.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',steps=['Compute the inclusive bounds 100−15=85 and 100+15=115.','Test each candidate against those bounds.'],assumptions=['The source’s ±15 is an example; this question explicitly defines an inclusive scalar rule.'])
add([1432,1433],'erosion-rule','Three-by-three erosion rule','With floor encoded as 1, a full 3×3 erosion keeps a center pixel as floor only when which condition holds?', ['All nine pixels in its 3×3 neighborhood are floor','At least one of the nine pixels is floor','A majority of the nine pixels are floor','Only the center pixel is floor'], 'Binary erosion with a full 3×3 structuring element requires every covered pixel to be foreground.', ['That is a dilation-like condition, not erosion.','A majority rule is a different nonlinear filter.','Erosion checks the whole neighborhood, not only its center.'])
add(1433,'erosion-area','Eroding a rectangular floor patch','A 7×5 all-floor rectangle is surrounded by non-floor pixels; after one full 3×3 erosion, how many floor pixels remain?', ['15','35','24','3'], 'One border layer is removed from every side, leaving (7−2)×(5−2)=5×3=15.', ['This is the original area without erosion.','This does not match removal of one layer on all four sides.','This counts only one remaining dimension rather than the area.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Shrink width from 7 to 5.','Shrink height from 5 to 3.','Multiply to obtain 15 pixels.'],assumptions=['One erosion pass, full 3×3 kernel, zeros outside the stated rectangle.'])
add(1433,'erosion-noise','What floor-mask erosion can remove','Which mask artifact is directly removed by a full 3×3 erosion of floor=1?', ['An isolated floor pixel surrounded by non-floor pixels','Every hole inside a floor region, regardless of size','Every false non-floor pixel without shrinking floor','Every calibration error in the camera model'], 'An isolated foreground pixel lacks the required all-floor neighborhood and disappears.', ['Erosion does not fill holes; it can expand them.','Erosion shrinks foreground and does not remove all false negatives.','Morphology changes a mask, not camera calibration.'],role='premise')
add([1433,1434],'ray-boundary','Stopping at the first non-floor pixel','Along a ray from the robot, the classified sequence is floor, floor, non-floor, floor, floor; which portion is treated as continuously traversable by the described rule?', ['Only the first two floor samples','All four floor samples regardless of the interruption','Only the last two floor samples','The three samples beginning with the non-floor sample'], 'The first non-floor sample ends the connected floor segment; later floor-like samples are ignored along that ray.', ['This would jump across the detected obstruction.','The later segment is disconnected by the intervening non-floor sample.','A non-floor sample is not part of the accepted floor segment.'],typ='scenario',role='premise')
add(1434,'metric-calibration','Pixel counts versus metric distance','Why is a calibration model or lookup table needed to turn a floor-mask boundary into obstacle distance?', ['Perspective makes vertical pixel position relate nonlinearly to ground distance','Every image row represents exactly one meter for any camera','Mask colors already contain an absolute distance in meters','Erosion directly outputs camera height and focal length'], 'Image coordinates alone are not metric distances; camera geometry supplies the required relationship.', ['Meters per row are not a universal constant.','The mask stores classes, not calibrated metric range.','Erosion does not estimate intrinsic or mounting parameters.'])
add(1435,'floor-depth','Worked floor-distance calculation','For a level pinhole camera 0.4 m above a flat floor, fy=500 pixels, v0=200 and a floor-contact boundary at v=250, what forward depth is z=fy h/(v−v0)?', ['4 m','0.004 m','2 m','10 m'], 'The numerator is 500×0.4=200 pixel-meters and the denominator is 50 pixels, giving 4 m.', ['This incorrectly introduces a factor of one thousand.','This uses a denominator of 100 rather than 50.','This drops the stated camera height factor.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Compute fy h=200 pixel-meters.','Compute v−v0=50 pixels.','Divide to obtain z=4 m.'],forms=[formula('z = fy*h/(v-v0)',{'z':'forward ground depth','fy':'vertical focal length','h':'camera height','v':'floor-contact image row','v0':'principal-point row'},{'z,h':'m','fy,v,v0':'pixels'},['Level camera optical axis, flat ground, pinhole model and matching pixel calibration.'])],assumptions=['The point is a floor-contact point, not an arbitrary upper point on an obstacle.'])
add(1435,'horizon-conflict','Equation horizon versus diagram label','Under v=v0+fy h/z with v0=200, what row does a flat-ground point approach as z tends to infinity?', ['v=200','v=300','v=400','v=0'], 'The fraction tends to zero, so the horizon for this level-camera equation is v0=200.', ['The figure labels row 300 as horizon, but that conflicts with the stated equation and v0.','Row 400 is the lower image boundary in the figure, not the equation’s limit.','Zero is the upper image boundary, not this calibrated horizon.'],typ='derivation',role='conflict',diff='medium',conflict='The figure labels v=300 as the horizon although its own level-camera equation and principal point imply v=200.',steps=['Let z grow without bound.','Then fy h/z tends to zero.','The limiting row is v0=200.'])
add(1435,'depth-order','Comparing ground depths','With the same level-camera parameters fy=500, h=0.4 and v0=200, which floor-contact row is farthest in forward depth?', ['v=250','v=270','v=290','v=300'], 'For rows below the horizon, z=200/(v−200); the smallest positive denominator gives the farthest depth.', ['This gives about 2.857 m, less than 4 m.','This gives about 2.222 m, less than 4 m.','This gives 2 m, less than 4 m.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',steps=['Compute positive row offsets 50,70,90,100.','Use inverse dependence on offset.','The offset 50 at row 250 gives the largest depth, 4 m.'])
add(1435,'offaxis','Forward depth versus lateral position','For the same camera with fx=fy=500, h=0.4, (u0,v0)=(320,200), a floor point is at (u,v)=(160,290); using z=fy h/(v−v0) and x=(u−u0)z/fx, what is (x,z)?', ['Approximately (−0.711,2.222) m','Approximately (0.711,2.222) m','Approximately (−160,2.222) m','Approximately (−0.711,4) m'], 'The depth is 200/90=20/9 m, and x=−160×(20/9)/500=−32/45 m.', ['The pixel lies left of the principal point, so x is negative in the stated convention.','A pixel offset is not itself a displacement in meters.','Four meters belongs to row 250, not row 290.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Compute z=500×0.4/(290−200)=20/9 m.','Compute u−u0=−160 pixels.','Compute x=−160×(20/9)/500=−32/45 m.'],assumptions=['Camera +x points right in the image; +z is forward.','The stated level-camera flat-ground model applies.'])
add(1435,'resized-calibration','Consistent calibration after halving resolution','If image coordinates and focal length are all halved, with fy=250, v0=100 and boundary v=125 while camera height stays 0.4 m, what depth results?', ['4 m','2 m','8 m','0.4 m'], 'The numerator and pixel offset both halve, so their ratio remains 4 m.', ['This halves the depth despite scaling both numerator and denominator.','This changes only one side of the ratio.','Height alone is not the forward depth.'],typ='numerical',role='premise',steps=['Compute 250×0.4=100.','Compute 125−100=25.','Compute 100/25=4 m.'])
add(1435,'depth-sensitivity','Distance sensitivity near the horizon','For z=fy h/(v−v0), what happens to sensitivity to a fixed row error as v approaches v0 from below in the image?', ['The magnitude of depth sensitivity grows because |dz/dv|=fy h/(v−v0)²','The sensitivity becomes a constant one meter per pixel','The sensitivity necessarily becomes zero','The distance becomes independent of focal length'], 'The denominator of the derivative shrinks, making depth estimates increasingly sensitive near the horizon.', ['The derivative depends on the row offset.','It grows rather than vanishes in this ideal model.','Focal length remains in both the depth and sensitivity expressions.'],typ='derivation',role='premise',diff='medium',steps=['Differentiate z=C/(v−v0) to get dz/dv=−C/(v−v0)².','Take the magnitude.','Observe that the squared denominator tends to zero.'],assumptions=['Below means v>v0 under the image’s downward-positive row convention.','The model is valid and v is not exactly v0.'])
add(1436,'override','Integrating Floor Finder with another planner','How is Floor Finder used when another navigation algorithm commands motion into a region classified as unsafe?', ['Modify or override that command using the local floor assessment','Require the robot to execute the command before checking the image','Replace the camera with the stored command sequence','Declare the destination unreachable without any further reasoning'], 'The described integration uses local perception to constrain or change a planned motion command.', ['The purpose is to react before committing to unsafe motion, not only afterward.','Commands are not a replacement for current observations.','A locally blocked direction does not by itself prove global unreachability.'])
add([1430,1436],'conditional-safety','Limits of the mapless safety claim','Why does a floor mask alone not establish guaranteed collision-free physical motion?', ['Errors, camera calibration, robot footprint, stopping distance and unobserved hazards still matter','A green pixel is always a certified free three-dimensional volume','A mapless controller cannot receive any other safety sensor','Every object sharing floor appearance is necessarily traversable'], 'An appearance classification is only one input to a physical safety decision and depends on several assumptions.', ['A pixel classification does not certify a full volume or robot clearance.','Mapless operation does not prohibit other sensors.','Similar appearance does not ensure physical traversability.'],role='conflict',conflict='The slides describe reliable safe regions and ensuring collision-free motion; those phrases do not prove safety under all real sensing and motion conditions.')
add([1432,1433,1434,1435,1436],'pipeline-synthesis','Tracing the complete floor-finding chain','Which sequence best matches the described use of a monocular image for local navigation?', ['Downsample/smooth → sample foreground floor → classify/refine mask → find first unsafe boundaries → apply calibrated distances → constrain motion','Classify mask → choose arbitrary metric distances → discard foreground sample → smooth motor commands only','Optimize a full pose graph → assume all pixels are floor → execute motion without a boundary check','Apply erosion to camera calibration → derive landmark identities → replace the image with a covariance matrix'], 'The method proceeds from appearance preprocessing and reference sampling to mask boundaries, calibrated interpretation and navigation response.', ['The reference and calibration cannot be replaced by arbitrary distances.','A full pose graph is not a required step of this mapless pipeline.','Erosion acts on a binary mask, not camera calibration or landmark identity.'],typ='scenario',role='premise',diff='medium')
